// Illustrative only — NOT used by this project.
//
// eve ships a built-in Twilio channel adapter that wires up SMS/MMS and a
// basic voice flow with a single import. For a proof-of-concept it's great:
//
//   import { twilioChannel } from "eve/channels/twilio";
//
//   export default twilioChannel({
//     allowFrom: "*",
//     messaging: { from: "*" },
//     async onText(ctx, message) {
//       await ctx.twilio.sendMessage(`Copy: ${message.body}`);
//       return null;
//     },
//   });
//
// We deliberately do NOT use it here. Once you push on it, you hit a ceiling:
//
//  - No inbound media. The parser reads Twilio's MediaUrl* fields but never
//    forwards them to the model, so MMS images are dropped.
//  - Voice is a workaround. <Gather> prompts a single utterance, terminates
//    the call, and feeds the transcript back as SMS. That's not a continuous
//    conversation.
//  - Session identity is per phone number. SMS from $TWILIO_SMS_NUMBER and
//    WhatsApp from $TWILIO_WHATSAPP_NUMBER get treated as two different
//    people, with two separate transcripts.
//  - You lose everything Twilio Conversations gives you. No Orchestrator
//    grouping, no Memory Store lookups, no Intelligence observations. The
//    adapter talks to the raw Twilio Messaging API, not the Conversations
//    platform.
//
// Instead, this project uses `twilio-orchestrator.ts`, which subscribes to
// Twilio Conversations Orchestrator webhooks (GROUP_BY_PROFILE) so SMS and
// WhatsApp from the same person land in one conversation, media is preserved,
// and Memory Store / Intelligence stay in the loop.
